````text
# #1700: Patch: Fix the Legendary/Mythic/Radiant sheen tearing
Opened by @MaxGoetzmann.

### What it does

A while back I asked you to implement a performance improving solution for the sheen across Legendary/Mythic/Radiant cards. While performance did increase, there's now an obvious dark triangle where the animation cycles. It's better than the first tear I asked you to fix, but still annoying.

Fix the non-smoothness while keeping performance of large hands/fields with this sheen intact.

### Acceptance

Performance degrades less than 5% on a full field and hand of cards with the sheen effect.

### Blocked by, part of

_No response_

### Difficulty (a guess)

Not sure (the planner rates it)

### Priority

low

### Who does it

The night bot (bot:build)

<!-- jackioh-bot:plan -->
## Plan

_Written by `claude-7` (claude, `opus`, strong) ([run](https://github.com/Jlore-Studios/JackiOh/actions/runs/39897555942)) before anyone built this. The night bot's builder starts from this section: edit it here to change the plan._

## Goal

The animated rarity sheen (the sweep on every Mythic face and every Radiant face, `data-foil="animated"`) stops drawing a dark triangle at a face's corner each time the band enters or leaves it, and keeps its cost on a full hand and field: one layer per face, moved by `transform` alone, with no extra work per frame. Done means the sweep's moving layer is composited without a blend mode, its band keeps about the brightness it has now, the still foil is unchanged, and `apps/web/src/cards/foil.test.ts` holds all of it.

**What the planner found (headless Chrome 141, ANGLE/SwiftShader GL, a copy of the sweep's CSS in `/tmp`, not in the repo):**
- The triangle shows only when a face is **rotated**, as every card in the home screen's fan is (`apps/web/src/routes/landing.css`, `.landing-fan-slot`, `rotate(±11deg, ±22deg)`). That is why it is "most evident on the home screen". It shows as a dark wedge past the rounded corner (top-left as the band comes in, bottom-right as it leaves, which is where the loop restarts) and a dark strip along the face's leading edge.
- It comes from `mix-blend-mode: overlay` on the moving layer. The same page with `mix-blend-mode: normal` draws nothing outside the clip and no dark strip. `screen` has the same wedges as `overlay`. Moving the sweep into `.cf-scale` behind a `clip-path` cut the band with hard straight lines.
- Cost: 40 animated faces at 1920×1080, frames drawn in 5 s (two runs each): no sweep 299/301; current overlay sweep 136/144; the same sweep with normal blending 172/180. Dropping the blend adds about 25% more frames, so the "< 5% degradation" acceptance holds with room to spare. (SwiftShader runs on the CPU, so these figures show the direction of the change, not a GPU's frame times.)

## Files to touch

| Path | Kind | What changes |
|---|---|---|
| `apps/web/src/cards/cards.css` | change | the foil section's animated sweep: `mix-blend-mode: normal`, lower stop alphas in the two `90deg` gradients, a sentence on #1700 in the sweep's comment |
| `apps/web/src/cards/foil.test.ts` | change | header line for #1700, helper `alphas`, constant `SWEPT_ALPHA_SHARE`, three new `it`s |
| `docs/polish/6-cards.md` | change | new last section "Addendum: the foil sweep's corners (2026-10-23, #1700)" |

Do not touch: `apps/web/src/cards/cosmetics.css` (the cosmetic foils), `apps/web/src/routes/landing.css`, any `.tsx`, `apps/web/src/cards/model.ts` (`foilFor`), the still foil's rule, the keyframes `cf-foil-sweep`, any crate, `spec/`, `crates/cards/patches/`. No spec section or ruling governs the sweep's look (it is presentation only, like #200's fix, which had no ruling), so add no ruling and no patch fragment.

